cartridge "RACE ACE/MIKE SEDORE/1990"

' RACE ACE
' A ColecoVision game based on "Race Ace" by Mike Sedore, COMPUTE!'s
' Gazette May 1990 (Commodore 64, machine language, SYS 2077). Made for
' Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy
' Bienvenu.
'
' The game: drive up a winding three-lane road against the clock. Push up
' to go faster, down to slow, a button brakes; left and right steer. The
' road bends a pixel a row; drive onto the kerb and you are slowed, run
' off it or hit a car and you crash. The time bar runs down;
' every crash costs a piece of it. The road goes through seven stretches:
' day, desert, day, night (only the lane lines and the other cars' tail
' lights show), day, ice (the car slides), day; then a checkpoint line.
' Crossing it pays 100 points for every piece of time left, fills the bar
' again, and the next lap's clock runs faster. One point every four rows.
' The game ends when the time bar is empty.
'
' From the original (orig/raceace.dis): speeds 13-117 MPH (the C64's delay
' 3-107, start 58 MPH), up +1 MPH a frame, down -1, the button -3 (its
' fire brake); the car's height on the screen follows the speed (faster is
' higher up); a kerb zone that costs 2 MPH a frame, a crash beyond it; a road that bends one pixel a row in runs of 4-35
' rows; lane dashes two rows on, two off; seven stretches of 512 rows in
' the C64's order (day, desert, day, night, day, ice, day) and a checkpoint
' line after them; a time bar of 71 pieces, one gone every 150 frames,
' a crash costs one; the checkpoint pays 100 points a piece, refills the
' bar, and makes a piece 20 frames shorter; one point every four rows;
' up to four other cars in the three lanes, slower and faster than you,
' entering at the top or the bottom, changing lanes now and then (never
' into a car), following the road; a crash puts you back in the middle at
' 58 MPH with the road cleared of cars; the ice's sideways slide; a high
' score for the session.
'
' Liberties: rebuilt for the ColecoVision's 256 x 192 screen (road 88
' pixels wide: 8-pixel kerbs, 24-pixel lanes, on 22 columns, the status on
' the right); the kerb slows the car but no longer pushes it back (on the
' C64 the push carried a car that was never steered round every bend, and
' here holding up alone finished laps); a scroll is drawn a frame later; the C64's loop ran as fast as it could, here the road moves
' MPH / 240 rows a frame (29 rows a second at 117 MPH, a lap of 3584 rows
' about 2.5 minutes at 100 MPH); bends turn left, straight or right with
' equal odds (the C64 favoured left); other cars run at 35-70 MPH, a car
' catching up with one ahead in its lane slows to its speed, a car never
' enters from the bottom under the player; roadside trees, cacti, firs and
' night reflectors are tiles (on the C64, sprites that could only be hit
' after leaving the road); a piece of time never gets shorter than 50
' frames; no attract mode; the white checkpoint line replaces the C64's
' one-character mark; engine sound on the third tone channel; title tune,
' title and credits pages are new.
'
' Controls: joystick only. Up faster, down slower, left/right steer,
' either side button brakes. Menus: up/down choose, a button starts. The
' title has START, HOW TO PLAY (three pages) and CREDITS.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` after every `clear
' sprites`. Own xorshift random numbers.

